- 1. An Authorization granted by the FAA which states that AASI PATH has been approved;
- 2. All interim Detailed Clinical Progress Notes (DCPN). At a MINIMUM, this must include: - a. EVERY 6 months (or more often as clinically indicated), progress notes from a clinic visit with a treating physician (MD/DO) or extender (PA-C/NP/midlevel); - b. At least one of these 6-month visits (and corresponding progress note) must come from a physician (MD/DO), such as a Primary Care Physician or Psychiatrist. - c. The most recent clinical progress note must be no more than 90 days before the AME exam; - d. Detailed Clinical Progress Notes must include a detailed interim summary; current medications, dosages, and side effects (if any); any changes in medication dose or type since last visit; physical exam findings; results of all testing performed; diagnosis; assessment and plan (prognosis); and follow-up; and
- 3. Anxiety, Depression, and Related Conditions PATH Status Summary completed by the treating physician and AME.

- Any NEW “Yes*” answers on the PATH Status Summary;
- Any new (or not previously reported) psychiatric symptoms, signs, or diagnosis;
- Any new history of substance use disorder or FAA substance dependence;
- Any progression (change in symptoms that persist despite modifications to medication and/or therapy) of the disease; and/or
- AME, treating physician, or treating provider has concerns.
